WEBVTT

00:00:05.972 --> 00:00:08.575
In this short
film, you will learn about how the brain

00:00:08.575 --> 00:00:11.711
and bladder connect
and work together to control bladder,

00:00:11.711 --> 00:00:18.819
empty

00:00:18.819 --> 00:00:20.120
the bladder stores urine

00:00:20.120 --> 00:00:24.057
until you are ready to empty it,
to urinate the bladder, send signals

00:00:24.057 --> 00:00:29.296
along the spinal cord to your brain
that it's filling up.

00:00:29.296 --> 00:00:30.130
This signals

00:00:30.130 --> 00:00:34.401
the feeling that you need to urinate

00:00:34.401 --> 00:00:36.336
when everything is working normally.

00:00:36.336 --> 00:00:39.940
You can then decide
when to empty your bladder.

00:00:39.940 --> 00:00:41.942
The bladder receives signals as it fills

00:00:41.942 --> 00:00:45.078
with urine to indicate
the need to urinate.

00:00:45.078 --> 00:00:48.381
When the bladder is about halfway
full, around 250

00:00:48.381 --> 00:00:52.686
milliliters, you get the first sensation
you need to urinate.

00:00:52.686 --> 00:00:56.490
As the bladder continues to fill,
the sensation becomes stronger

00:00:56.490 --> 00:01:00.460
and the urgency to urinate increases.

00:01:00.460 --> 00:01:02.295
The bladder muscles relax

00:01:02.295 --> 00:01:08.869
as your bladder fills,
but contract when you urinate.

00:01:08.869 --> 00:01:12.372
The sphincter muscle controls
flow of urine out of the bladder, which

00:01:12.372 --> 00:01:15.442
squeezes
the urethra shut as the bladder fills

00:01:15.442 --> 00:01:18.745
and relaxes as the bladder is empty.

00:01:18.745 --> 00:01:21.681
How often you need to urinate
depends on several things,

00:01:21.681 --> 00:01:25.352
such as your fluid intake, how much urine
you produce, how much you're

00:01:25.352 --> 00:01:27.420
in your bladder, comfortably hold.

00:01:27.420 --> 00:01:30.524
So that's a short explanation
of the connection between your brain

00:01:30.524 --> 00:01:34.524
and bladder and how they work together
when it comes to emptying your bladder.
